"""An app-started persona session that outlives the request that started it.

`session.ask()` is one HTTP request, one subprocess, one stream: the request
handler owns the whole thing, and the process dies when the handler returns
or the client disconnects. That is wrong for a session the APP starts —
"a document arrives and the Fat Controller starts reading it" — because
nothing asked a question and nobody is necessarily watching
(`docs/design/agent-jobs.md`). This module is that other shape: an in-process
`asyncio.Task`, independent of any request, whose every event is persisted as
it lands (`jobs_models.py`) and fanned out live to whoever is watching
(`_JobBus`) — so a caller can open the job at any time, from the start if it
already finished or caught up and then live if it is still running, on the
SAME verbatim event stream `session.ask()` already produces.

No queue, no worker process, no new service: the task IS the job, held alive
by nothing but this module's own `_tasks` dict (an unreferenced `asyncio.Task`
is eligible for garbage collection mid-run — the classic "fire and forget"
trap), and it keeps running for exactly as long as the process it wraps does,
whether or not any request is in flight and whether or not anyone is watching
(`start()`'s docstring; `docs/design/agent-jobs.md` §Constraints).

`--bg`/`claude agents` was measured and is not this: it is the CLI's own
interactive-session dispatcher, spawning a worktree and refusing `--print`
outright ("--bg and --print conflict: --print never starts the interactive
session that `claude agents` attaches to"). A job here answers a structured
question or reads a document from a working folder, headless, exactly the
`-p --output-format stream-json` shape `session.py` already drives — a
different CLI feature for a different job.

A job belongs to a workspace (`AgentJob`/`AgentJobEvent` are `WorkspaceScoped`
— `docs/design/agent-jobs.md` §Tenancy, added on review after the first cut of
this slice gated only on the persona entitlement and let any caller who held
it read or steer any workspace's job). Every session this module opens is
created OUTSIDE a request — `db_session_factory()` is the app's plain,
engine-bound sessionmaker, never `SessionDep`'s request-scoped one, because a
job's writes must outlive the one request that triggered them — so nothing
binds it to a workspace the way `CurrentWorkspace` binds a request's own
session. Every function below that touches the database therefore calls
`db.scoping.bind_session()` on its own freshly-opened session BEFORE it
touches `AgentJob`/`AgentJobEvent`, using the `workspace_id`/`created_by_id`
its caller supplied. `authorize()` is the one exception, reading with
`unscoped()` because the caller's workspace is exactly what it is deciding
whether to trust.
"""

